There were 5,586,835 private sector businesses in the UK at the start of 2026, which the release rounds to 5.6 million. The source is the [Business population estimates 2026](https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/business-population-estimates-2026/business-population-estimates-for-the-uk-and-regions-2026-statistical-release), published on 5 November 2026 by the Department for Business, Innovation, Science and Trade. The count is 104,000 (1.8%) lower than the 2025 release.

_Last checked: 24 November 2026. Every figure below was read from the publication it links to. These are estimates of the private sector, not a register count._

## At a glance

1. **5,586,835 private sector businesses at the start of 2026.** Table C of the 2026 release. The 2025 release gave 5,690,265 ([2026 release](https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/business-population-estimates-2026/business-population-estimates-for-the-uk-and-regions-2026-statistical-release), [2025 release](https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/business-population-estimates-2025/business-population-estimates-for-the-uk-and-regions-2025-statistical-release)).
2. **74% have no employees.** That is 4,115,175 businesses with no employees against 1,471,660 with at least one.
3. **99.85% are SMEs.** The release counts 5,578,480 businesses with 0 to 249 employees and 8,355 large businesses with 250 or more.
4. **SMEs hold 60% of employment and 50% of turnover.** That is 16,980 thousand of 28,323 thousand jobs and £2.78 trillion of turnover. Turnover excludes finance and insurance.
5. **The fall since 2025 is inside the margin of error.** The 95% range is 5,478,000 to 5,695,000, and the release says "we cannot confidently state there has been a fall in the real business population".

{% stats %}
{% stat value="5,586,835" label="private sector businesses, start of 2026" source="Business population estimates 2026, Table C" /%}
{% stat value="74%" label="have no employees (4,115,175)" source="Business population estimates 2026" /%}
{% stat value="99.85%" label="are SMEs (5,578,480)" source="Business population estimates 2026" /%}
{% /stats %}

The release calls itself "the only official estimate of the total number of private sector businesses in the UK at the start of each year". It is an estimate, not a count. It combines a business register with a survey-based estimate of businesses that are not registered for VAT or PAYE.

## Which figure can I cite?

Use the row that matches your sentence. Quote the base and the date with the figure.

| Figure | What it counts | Date | Source | Published |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **5,586,835** | All UK private sector businesses, registered and unregistered | Start of 2026 | [Business population estimates 2026, Table C](https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/business-population-estimates-2026/business-population-estimates-for-the-uk-and-regions-2026-statistical-release) | 5 November 2026 |
| **4,115,175** (74%) | Private sector businesses with no employees | Start of 2026 | Same release, Table C and text | 5 November 2026 |
| **5,578,480** (99.85%) | Private sector SMEs, 0 to 249 employees | Start of 2026 | Same release, Table C and text | 5 November 2026 |
| **28,323 thousand** | Employment across private sector businesses | Start of 2026 | Same release, Table C. The release calls its employment estimates indicative | 5 November 2026 |
| **£5,544,518 million** | Turnover of private sector businesses, excluding finance and insurance (SIC 2007 Section K) | Start of 2026 | Same release, Table C | 5 November 2026 |
| **2,786,985** | Businesses registered for VAT and/or PAYE, including government and non-profit bodies | March 2026 (IDBR snapshot of 13 March 2026) | [ONS, UK business; activity, size and location: 2026, Table 1](https://www.ons.gov.uk/businessindustryandtrade/business/activitysizeandlocation/bulletins/ukbusinessactivitysizeandlocation/2026) | 24 September 2026 |

Safe wording follows this pattern: "There were 5,586,835 private sector businesses in the UK at the start of 2026 (Business population estimates 2026, Department for Business, Innovation, Science and Trade, published 5 November 2026)."

## How many UK businesses are there by size?

Most are one-person businesses. The 4,115,175 with no employees are 74% of the total, and only 8,355 businesses have 250 or more employees.

| Size band | Businesses | Employment (thousands) | Turnover (£ millions) |
|---|---|---|---|
| All private sector businesses | **5,586,835** | 28,323 | 5,544,518 |
| No employees | 4,115,175 | 4,474 | 355,565 |
| 1 to 9 employees | 1,198,870 | 4,287 | 704,436 |
| 10 to 49 employees | 225,215 | 4,421 | 752,094 |
| 50 to 249 employees (medium) | 39,220 | 3,798 | 964,651 |
| 250 or more employees (large) | 8,355 | 11,344 | 2,767,773 |
| SMEs, 0 to 249 employees | 5,578,480 | 16,980 | 2,776,745 |

Source: [Business population estimates 2026, Table C](https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/business-population-estimates-2026/business-population-estimates-for-the-uk-and-regions-2026-statistical-release), start of 2026. Turnover excludes SIC 2007 Section K.

{% chart type="bar" eyebrow="Start of 2026" title="UK private sector businesses by number of employees" data="No employees:4115175|1 to 9:1198870|10 to 49:225215|50 to 249:39220|250 or more:8355" source="Business population estimates 2026, Table C, published 5 November 2026" /%}

The word "small" needs care. The release uses "small" for the whole 0 to 49 band, which is 5,539,260 businesses (99.15%) and includes the 4,115,175 with no employees. Its Table A also labels 10 to 49 employees as "small" and 1 to 9 as "micro". Say which band you mean. The other bands in the release are 39,220 medium-sized businesses (0.70%) and 8,355 large businesses (0.15%).

"No employees" is not the same as "sole trader". The release's no-employee group comprises sole proprietorships and partnerships with only self-employed owner-managers, plus companies with one employee, assumed to be a working proprietor. Of the 4,115,175, the detailed tables put 2,890,875 as unregistered and 1,224,300 as registered for VAT and/or PAYE ([Table 1](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6ae3162ec591dbfc31fb67f0/BPE_2026_detailed_tables.xlsx)).

Almost three in four UK businesses have no employees. My [small business SEO consultant](/services/small-business-seo-consultant/) page sets out how I work with small businesses.

## Employment and turnover by business size

SMEs are 99.85% of businesses and 60% of private sector employment. Large businesses are 0.15% of businesses and 40% of employment.

| Size band | Employment (thousands) | Share of employment | Turnover (£ millions) | Share of turnover |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Small, 0 to 49 employees | 13,182 | 47% | 1,812,095 | 33% |
| Medium, 50 to 249 employees | 3,798 | 13% | 964,651 | 17% |
| Large, 250 or more employees | 11,344 | 40% | 2,767,773 | 50% |
| All SMEs | 16,980 | 60% | 2,776,745 | 50% |
| All private sector businesses | 28,323 | 100% | 5,544,518 | 100% |

Source: [Business population estimates 2026](https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/business-population-estimates-2026/business-population-estimates-for-the-uk-and-regions-2026-statistical-release), Table C and the text on composition. Turnover excludes SIC 2007 Section K (financial and insurance activities), "where turnover is not available on a comparable basis".

{% chart type="bar" series="Employment;Turnover" eyebrow="Start of 2026" title="Share of employment and turnover by business size band" data="Small (0 to 49):47;33|Medium (50 to 249):13;17|Large (250 or more):40;50" suffix="%" source="Business population estimates 2026, Table C, published 5 November 2026. Turnover excludes SIC 2007 Section K." /%}

The release reports SME turnover as £2.78 trillion (50%) and total turnover as £5,544,518 million. It also warns that its employment and turnover figures are indicative. It points to the ONS Labour Market Statistics for employment and the ONS Annual Business Survey for turnover.

## Did the number of UK businesses fall in 2026?

The estimate is lower, but the release does not state that the real population fell. The 2026 total is 104,000 (1.8%) below 2025. The release gives a 95% range for 2026 of 5,478,000 to 5,695,000 and for 2025 of 5,567,000 to 5,813,000. Those ranges overlap, which is my reading of the two published ranges.

| Measure | 2025 release | 2026 release | Change as the release states it |
|---|---|---|---|
| All private sector businesses | 5,690,265 | 5,586,835 | Decreased by 104,000 (1.8%) |
| No employees | 4,272,535 | 4,115,175 | Decreased by 157,000 (3.7%) |
| Employers (1 or more employees) | 1,417,730 | 1,471,660 | Increased by 54,000 (3.8%) |
| SMEs (0 to 249) | 5,681,930 | 5,578,480 | Not stated |
| Medium (50 to 249) | 38,435 | 39,220 | Not stated |
| Large (250 or more) | 8,335 | 8,355 | Not stated |
| Employment (thousands) | 28,128 | 28,323 | Increased by 0.7% |
| SME employment (thousands) | 16,882 | 16,980 | Increased by 0.6% |
| Turnover (£ millions) | 5,524,662 | 5,544,518 | Not stated |
| SME share of turnover | 51% | 50% | Not stated |
| Registered for VAT and/or PAYE | 46% | 48% | Registered businesses increased by 51,000 (1.9%) |

Sources: [2026 release](https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/business-population-estimates-2026/business-population-estimates-for-the-uk-and-regions-2026-statistical-release) and [2025 release](https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/business-population-estimates-2025/business-population-estimates-for-the-uk-and-regions-2025-statistical-release), Table C, Table A and text. The two releases are published by different departments under different names.

The exact Table C difference between 5,690,265 and 5,586,835 is 103,430. That is my own subtraction. The release rounds it to 104,000.

The movement sits in the non-employing group. Non-employing businesses fell by 157,000 while employing businesses rose by 54,000. The release attributes the fall in non-employers to a decrease of 154,000 (5.1%) in unregistered businesses and 3,000 (0.3%) in registered non-employers. Unregistered numbers are survey estimates, so the release says changes "may reflect real changes in the population, or may be due to sampling error".

The 2025 release moved the other way. It reported the population up by 191,000 (3.5%) on 2024. Do not describe a single year as a trend.

## The long-run series, 2010 to 2026

The count was 4.5 million in 2010 and peaked at around 6.0 million at the start of 2020. The release says business numbers have fluctuated around 5.6 million since 2021.

{% chart type="line" eyebrow="Start of each year" title="UK private sector businesses, 2010 to 2026" data="2010:4483000|2011:4588500|2012:4817900|2013:4914400|2014:5247000|2015:5401200|2016:5498000|2017:5694500|2018:5667500|2019:5867800|2020:5980500|2021:5590900|2022:5508900|2023:5555100|2024:5499000|2025:5690300|2026:5586800" source="Business population estimates 2026, detailed Table 25a, published 5 November 2026. Table 25a rounds to the nearest 100." /%}

The release states these changes:

- **2010 to 2026:** the population increased by 1.1 million (24.6%). Non-employing businesses accounted for 78% of the increase.
- **2020 to 2026:** the population decreased by 394,000 (6.6%). Employing businesses increased by 59,000 (4.2%) and non-employing businesses decreased by 453,000 (9.9%).
- **Largest year-on-year change since 2010:** an increase of 6.8% between 2013 and 2014 and a decrease of 6.5% between 2020 and 2021.

## UK businesses by legal form

Sole proprietorships are the most common legal form. At the start of 2026 the private sector comprised 3,082,040 sole proprietorships, 2,161,860 companies and 342,935 ordinary partnerships.

| Legal form | Businesses, 2026 | Share, 2026 | With employees, 2026 | No employees, 2026 | Share, 2025 | Change 2025 to 2026 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Sole proprietorships | 3,082,040 | 55% | 175,000 | 2.9 million | 57% | Decreased by 137,000 (4%) |
| Companies | 2,161,860 | 39% | 1.22 million | 943,000 | 37% | Increased by 58,000 (3%) |
| Ordinary partnerships | 342,935 | 6% | 77,000 | 265,000 | 6% | Decreased by 25,000 (7%) |

Sources: [2026 release](https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/business-population-estimates-2026/business-population-estimates-for-the-uk-and-regions-2026-statistical-release) text and Table B; exact 2026 counts from [detailed Table 3](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6ae3162ec591dbfc31fb67f0/BPE_2026_detailed_tables.xlsx); 2025 shares from the [2025 release](https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/business-population-estimates-2025/business-population-estimates-for-the-uk-and-regions-2025-statistical-release). Companies are actively trading companies. The release assumes no unregistered company is an active business.

The release also splits the population by registration. ONS recorded 2.7 million private sector businesses as registered for VAT and/or PAYE (48%) and 2.9 million (52%) as unregistered. The registered share was 44% in 2020. About 377,000 sole proprietorships (12%) and 157,000 ordinary partnerships (46%) were registered.

## UK businesses by industry

SMEs make up at least 99% of the businesses in each main industry. The release reports SME counts by industry, not the all-business count, so the table shows SMEs.

| Industry | SMEs, 2025 release | SMEs, 2026 release |
|---|---|---|
| Construction | 885,000 (16%) | 846,000 (15%) |
| Professional, scientific and technical | 819,000 (14%) | 778,000 (14%) |
| Wholesale and retail trade | 547,000 (10%) | 523,000 (9%) |
| Mining, quarrying and utilities | 28,000 (under 1%) | 35,000 (under 1%) |

Sources: [2026 release](https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/business-population-estimates-2026/business-population-estimates-for-the-uk-and-regions-2026-statistical-release) and [2025 release](https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/business-population-estimates-2025/business-population-estimates-for-the-uk-and-regions-2025-statistical-release), industries section. Shares are of all SMEs.

{% chart type="bar" series="2025 release;2026 release" eyebrow="SMEs by industry" title="UK private sector SMEs in four industries, 2025 and 2026 releases" data="Construction:885000;846000|Professional, scientific and technical:819000;778000|Wholesale and retail trade:547000;523000|Mining, quarrying and utilities:28000;35000" source="Business population estimates 2025 (2 October 2025) and 2026 (5 November 2026), industries section" /%}

Other 2026 industry figures from the release:

- **Total business numbers, 2025 to 2026:** the greatest numeric increase was in other service activities (14,000). The greatest numeric decrease was in professional, scientific and technical activities (41,000).
- **Wholesale and retail trade:** 14% of SME employment and 31% of SME turnover.
- **Accommodation and food service:** 9.5% of SME employment and 3.1% of SME turnover.
- **Large businesses:** manufacturing had the most (1,245), then administrative and support service activities (1,170) and wholesale and retail trade (1,150).

Industry estimates carry more uncertainty than the total. The release gives coefficients of variation of 2% for construction, wholesale and retail trade, and professional, scientific and technical activities, and 11% for mining, quarrying and utilities. It also says the 2026 confidence intervals overlap the 2025 intervals for all sector breakdowns.

